:answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186068218}] :question/id #uuid "289f43cd-07b8-54ff-8ce2-696ccf1de6b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-7254" :question/problem "![](images/91/9161d393a05baa44af91912eb1fde5c367e9e33e6671150a65e90e21bbfef14a.png)\n\nThe graph above shows the curve $y = (\\frac{1}{5})^{x} + c$. Determine the value of $c$ and find the equation of the curve."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31aed7e5-e5d2-5541-b5a4-8ee111458175" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342658} {:answer/id #uuid "53fe78ad-4e06-5c02-ba60-d2a3ed4a806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342936} {:answer/id #uuid "94b23f54-2e5c-5882-a561-8b4c48e54ab7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343536} {:answer/id #uuid "e08600a0-a8a5-5e6b-8ef4-f108728ce564" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344208} {:answer/id #uuid "e101c708-804d-5a6c-9d93-3f2cb6c5c774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53fe78ad-4e06-5c02-ba60-d2a3ed4a806e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342936} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5839a8-10f3-59f1-a4ce-4a9812ab93d3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344770}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aff1d338-551f-5fdf-bfe8-0a79b83891e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-75585" :question/problem "Which value of $x$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{1}{x}=\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $x=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $x,2x,$ and $4x.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(x,2x,4x)=4x.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $4x,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{x} & =\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x} \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x}+\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =4x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4x\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) & =2\\cdot 2x\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{2x})+4x\\cdot (\\frac{3}{4x}) \\\\ 4\\cancel{x}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{x}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{2x}\\cdot (\\frac{2+x}{\\cancel{2x}})+\\cancel{4x}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{4x}}) \\\\ 4\\cdot (1) & =2\\cdot (2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =2(2+x)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $x,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =2(2+x)+3 \\\\ 4 & =4+2x+3 \\\\ 2x & =-3 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $x=0$ as a solution. But since $x=-\\frac{3}{2}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $x=-\\frac{3}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186069532 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "121b603b-942f-556e-b445-2d681ee1ce18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348602} {:answer/id #uuid "500546dd-32e0-5461-ae4c-5e5a7455b919" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={x}^{2}+x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349173} {:answer/id #uuid "6e22fd1e-737c-5a35-9366-28fb7f8a36bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+x)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349458} {:answer/id #uuid "da4666f1-e8ca-5840-a41a-ead889cfbef8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=({x}^{2}+x+C)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350361} {:answer/id #uuid "f06bf25f-3d03-5f76-a7ea-8ddc6224884a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350551}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "121b603b-942f-556e-b445-2d681ee1ce18" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348602} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e1d264b-d2af-5bb5-bbc0-5706b21c43ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "679701a5-b0c3-5865-adfd-e49e046da668" :question/math-academy-id "q-8303" :question/problem "Find the general solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y}=2x\\sqrt{y}$ for $y\\ne 0.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the general solution, we first separate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\sqrt{y} & =2x\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =2x\\sqrt{y}+\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =(2x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we integrate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{y}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int (2x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ 2\\sqrt{y} & ={x}^{2}+x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to the differential equation (in implicit form) is\n\n$$\n2\\sqrt{y}={x}^{2}+x+C\n$$\n\nWe're required to write the solution in the form $y=y(x).$ By doing this, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}+x+C}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073607 :question/id #uuid "715caf63-b974-519f-b0ff-b8fe9a8eb591" :question/math-academy-id "e-3015" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\overline{AB} \\parallel \\overline{EF}$, $\\overline{BC} \\parallel \\overline{DE}$, $AD = 4$, $AB = 6$, and $BF = 2$.\nFind the measure of $\\overline{CF}$.\n\n![](images/5b/5b445bd5f7bd44956d499b18c0aa29842c77879a301ec46c8d1a49dcb4c2fe34.png)" :question/worked-solution "The triangle proportionality theorem states the following:\n\n> If a line parallel to a side of a triangle intersects the other two sides, then the line divides those sides proportionally.\n\nFirst, since $AD = 4$ and\n\n$$\nAD+DB=AB = 6\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\nDB = AB-AD = 6-4=2\n$$\n\nNow, we apply the triangle proportionality theorem twice:\n\n- Since $\\overline{DE} \\parallel \\overline{BC}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{AD}{DB} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= \\frac{4}{2} \\\\\n\\frac{AE}{EC} &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Since $\\overline{EF} \\parallel \\overline{AB}$, then by the triangle proportionality theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= \\frac{CE}{EA} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{FB} &= (\\frac{AE}{EC})^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= 2^{-1} \\\\\n\\frac{CF}{2} &= \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\nCF &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074258 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b5dccf36-4825-59b6-b1b2-d6636da14427" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071787} {:answer/id #uuid "cba0e5fd-0edf-5a1a-b242-bf86f67d2feb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/re