" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071796} {:answer/id #uuid "45b984e7-c2b3-5907-ba6b-b9e2ee31653a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071797}] :answer-field/id #uuid "701338d2-8d36-587b-a4b0-1b582156d8e3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071792}] :question/id #uuid "f97dc55a-e461-5086-8c03-0ce0f72b1291" :question/math-academy-id "q-54369" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $\\sin 20^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{π}{9})$\n2. $\\sin 50^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{2π}{9})$\n3. $\\sin 80^{∘} = \\sin (\\frac{4π}{9})$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074260 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "950db3a0-26a4-573f-97cd-8af8939867b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071799} {:answer/id #uuid "a6ffdd69-2209-59ca-9cc9-fcb8627f9a29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071800} {:answer/id #uuid "c04834d0-3006-5723-995d-d3e4ccc32f4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071801} {:answer/id #uuid "d1396e3c-cad3-5598-8a1c-4897ac66dc1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071802} {:answer/id #uuid "20f60a68-cab8-5512-9a74-43255342d90e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071803}] :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c92903-d98b-539b-a664-d6d24af4d546" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071798}] :question/id #uuid "a864902c-95ec-5b3c-b55b-9dc6a4d563e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-54376" :question/problem "What is $\\cos (\\frac{π}{3})$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074263 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "819902c3-0b4c-5fe3-8521-1feb1f1898c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071817} {:answer/id #uuid "839d15c6-6d7c-56ff-8881-c319c5591795" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071818} {:answer/id #uuid "9264484d-8af9-5098-9c32-2cb2d1800d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071819} {:answer/id #uuid "f99ccf96-680a-5706-8358-6370757366d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071820} {:answer/id #uuid "607426dd-b178-50a9-88aa-a314d7b6e7f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071821}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9264484d-8af9-5098-9c32-2cb2d1800d45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071819} :answer-field/id #uuid "26c10b88-71f5-5390-8e7b-29b04e15ed55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186071816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f010bc2-8739-563b-8590-b9a0b63c41ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-54384" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we convert $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians to an equivalent measure in degrees. This gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi })=\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{4}={45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, since we know that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡{45}^{∘}=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078147 :question/id #uuid "175a3e1e-2f85-555b-a0df-17e9d71f1f6d" :question/math-academy-id "e-12666" :question/problem "Prove that $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ using mathematical induction." :question/worked-solution "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1), P(2), P(3),\\ldots,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)\\Rightarrow P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in\\mathbb N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^n \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that they're equal:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}}=\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}}=\\frac{1}{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side,  we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{1}})=\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{3}{4})=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n = k \\geq 1.$ \n\nDue to the base case, we already *know* that the statement is true for *some* value of $k,$ namely $k = 1,$ so this assumption is valid.\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n = k \\geq 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^k}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k) \\Rightarrow P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**, so let's write that, too.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^{k+1}}\\right). \\qquad\\qquad (\\ast)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this equals the right-hand side.\n\nWhen proving sums of finite series using induction, the key to unlocking the proof is to write the last term on its own, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\sum_{i=1}^{k+1} a_i =  \\left(\\sum_{i=1}^{k} a_i\\right) + a_{k+1}\n$$\n\nLet's apply this technique in this case:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{k+1}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}} & =({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({\\sum }_{i=1}^{k}\\frac{1}{{4}^{i}})+(\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}) & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}})+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k}}+\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{12}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k}}(\\frac{1}{3\\cdot 4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}-\\frac{1}{3\\cdot {4}^{k+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(1-\\frac{1}{{4}^{k+1}}),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(\\ast)$ equals the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^{n} \\dfrac{1}{4^i} = \\dfrac{1}{3}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{1}{4^n}\\right)$ is true for all $n\\geq 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078166 :question/id #uuid "31f9d893-26fd-5b2e-9730-57c5e6cc0615" :question/math-academy-id "e-12857" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{2n+6}{3} = 4n+1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying both sides by $3.$ We must make sure that *all* of the terms on the right-hand side are multiplied by $3.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2n+6}{3} & =4n+1 \\\\ 3\\cdot (\\frac{2n+6}{3}) & =3\\cdot (4n+1) \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{2n+6}{\\cancel{3}}) & =3\\cdot 4n+3\\cdot 1 \\\\ 2n+6 & =12n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+6 & =12n+3 \\\\ 2n+6-2n & =12n+3-2n \\\\ 6 & =10n+3 \\\\ 6-3 & =10n+3-3 \\\\ 3 & =10n \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =\\frac{10n}{10} \\\\ \\frac{3}{10} & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $n = \\dfrac{3}{10}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078201 :question/id #uuid "f25cd97e-0a26-5b3b-a4f7-aef8acb561a1" :question/math-academy-id "e-1505" :question/problem "Which of the sequences below are geometric sequences?\n\nI. $2, \\: 4, \\: 8, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$\n\nII. $1, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{1}{9}, \\: -\\dfrac{1}{27}, \\: \\ldots$\n\nIII. $4, \\: 8, \\: 12, \\: 16, \\: \\ldots$" :question/worked-solution "A sequence is geometric if there is a common ratio between its terms. Let's check each sequence in turn.\n\n- Sequence I is geometric. For each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{2} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{16}{8} & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence II is also geometric. Again, for each pair of consecutive terms, the common ratio is the same:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{3})}{1} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{(\\frac{1}{9})}{(-\\frac{1}{3})} & =-\\frac{1}{3} \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{3}}=\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{27})}{(\\frac{1}{9})} & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Sequence III is not geometric because not every pair of consecutive terms has the same ratio. To see this, we only need to go up to the third term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{4} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{12}{8} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn conclusion, only sequences I and II could be geometric sequences."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078204 :question/id #uuid "4bf89b8f-f713-51b5-8536-5aa5b08f16d3" :question/math-academy-id "e-1511" :question/problem "Find the formula for the $n$th term of a sequence if the $6$th term is $80$ and the common ratio is $-2.$" :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=-2,$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term $a_1.$ We know that the $6$th term is $a_6=80,$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{6} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{6-1} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{5} \\\\ 80 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-32) \\\\ -\\frac{80}{32} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2} & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is $a_1=-\\dfrac{5}{2},$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{2}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify a bit further by manipulating the exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-\\frac{5}{2}(-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-2)^{n-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078206 :question/id #uuid "e3fed3e8-9bc6-558d-a426-96006871a20d" :question/math-academy-id "e-1516" :question/problem "Given that a geometric sequence has $a_2 = 24$, $a_5 = 3$ and $a_k = 12$, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the common ratio. We are told that $a_2=24$ and $a_5=3,$ so we can find the common ratio using a formula, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{3}{24} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the common ratio is $r=\\dfrac{1}{2}$, we can use the same formula to solve for the value of $k$ such that $a_k=12\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{k-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{k}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{12}{24} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {(\\frac{1}{2})}^{k-2} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1} \\\\ k-2 & =1 \\\\ k & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078239 :question/id #uuid "4bb9f4f9-72db-5acc-8628-25ef5848163a" :question/math-academy-id "e-295" :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\sqrt{49.1}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ at $x=49$." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of $f(x)$ at the point $x = a$ is\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(a) + f'(a)(x-a)\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=49$, which gives\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(49) + f^{′}(49)(x - 49)\n$$\n\nLet's compute $f(49)$ and $f'(49)$ in the expression above. Evaluating the function, we get\n\n$$\nf\\left(49\\right) = \\sqrt{49}= 7\n$$\n\nand computing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\sqrt{x}) = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(49\\right) = \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt{49}} = \\dfrac{1}{2\\cdot 7} = \\dfrac{1}{14}\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\nL(x) = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(x - 49)\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\sqrt{49.1}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=49.1$. We get\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} \\mid \\approx L(49.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(49.1 - 49); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(0.1); = 7 + \\frac{1}{14}(\\frac{1}{10}); = 7 + \\frac{1}{140}; = \\frac{981}{140}; \\approx 7.007143\n$$\n\nNote that the exact answer, rounded to six decimal places, is\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{49.1} = 7.007\\,139\n$$\n\nOur approximation is very close!"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078241 :question/id #uuid "73ca2ce4-236d-557b-be51-2eb1be252796" :question/math-academy-id "e-297" :question/problem "What is the tangent line approximation of $\\tan x$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$?" :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\tan x\n$$\n\nat\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) + f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\tan\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 1\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\tan x) = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = \\sec^2 \\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right) = 2\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= f(\\frac{π}{4}) + f^{′}(\\frac{π}{4})(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 1 + 2(x - \\frac{π}{4}) \\\\\n&= 2x + 1 - \\frac{π}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078242 :question/id #uuid "33945d7c-21a4-56e2-aa35-690003827fa7" :question/math-academy-id "e-298" :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\cos{89^{\\circ}}$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x) = \\cos x$ at $x=90^\\circ$." :question/worked-solution "Remember that the derivative of any trigonometric function is always expressed in radians. For that reason, we need to convert all degree units to radians.\n\nTo convert an angle expressed in degrees to an equivalent angle in radians, we multiply the angle in degrees by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ}\n$$\n\nThis gives,\n\n$$\n90^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n89^\\circ \\cdot \\dfrac{\\pi}{180^\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nThe tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\cos x\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na = 90^\\circ = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) +f'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\\left(x-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = 0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\nf'(x) = \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}}{\\textrm{d}x}(\\cos x) = -\\sin x\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 + (-1)(x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $\\cos{89^{\\circ}}$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\na = 89^{\\circ} = \\dfrac{89\\pi}{180}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos 89^{∘} \\mid \\approx L(\\frac{89π}{180}); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{89π}{180}; = \\frac{π}{180}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078248 :question/id #uuid "b05d1090-fd7b-52eb-84a7-55377ead0dda" :question/math-academy-id "e-3229" :question/problem "Find the center and radius of the circle whose equation is $x^2 + y^2 - x  = 6.$" :question/worked-solution "To find the center and radius of the circle, we need to group $x$ and $y$ terms and complete the squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-x & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-x)+{y}^{2} & =6 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =6+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2} \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2})+{y}^{2} & =\\frac{25}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can factor the perfect square and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+{y}^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2} \\\\ {(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+(y-0)^{2} & ={(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the circle is centered at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}, 0 \\right)$ and its radius is $\\dfrac{5}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078265 :question/id #uuid "d2f40096-4001-5cd9-aeda-52c5894649b4" :question/math-academy-id "e-3261" :question/problem "Find $\\sec L$ for $\\triangle KLJ$ (shown below).\n\n![](images/c0/c08613b38502628d71ffa0cdac65400420fb25926a64b747b925ee06d6d560ca.png)" :question/worked-solution "Remember that $\\sec$ is the reciprocal of $\\cos.$ So first we need to compute cosine:\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{\\text{adjacent}}{\\text{hypotenuse}} = \\dfrac{JL}{KL} = \\dfrac{JL}{5}\n$$\n\nTo find the length $JL,$ we can use the Pythagorean Theorem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}JL & =\\sqrt{K{L}^{2}-J{K}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{5}^{2}-{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25-9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\cos L = \\dfrac{JL}{5} = \\dfrac{4}{5},\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡L & ={(\\frac{4}{5})}^{-1}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078332 :question/id #uuid "b5a4461e-fda6-50d2-98df-0c4937cc3e50" :question/math-academy-id "e-626" :question/problem "The velocity $\\mathbf{v}$ at time $t$ of a particle $P$ moving in the $xy$-plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}(t) = \\langle \\cos t, \\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nGiven that the particle is at the position  $\\mathbf{r}=\\left\\langle 0,\\, 0\\right\\rangle$ when $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}2,$ calculate the position of $P$ at time $t=\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the position vector, we need to integrate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =\\int v(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =⟨\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{C}_{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can solve for the constants of integration using the fact that $\\mathbf{r}\\left( \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\right) = \\langle 0,0 \\rangle.$ Substituting $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{1},\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+{C}_{2}⟩ \\\\ ⟨0,0⟩ & =⟨1+{C}_{1},\\,0+{C}_{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso we must have $C_1=-1$ and $C_2 = 0.$ Therefore, the position is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the position at time $t=\\pi$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -1,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0-1,\\,-(-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078336 :question/id #uuid "0fba12b1-fa78-5ff2-88e4-2a690105d321" :question/math-academy-id "e-6571" :question/problem "Given that $D=x^2\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}}{\\textrm d x^{3}}-5x \\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}}{\\textrm d x^{2}} +9\\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d x},$ what is $D(y)$ for $y=\\ln{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "The given operator is a third-order differential operator of the form\n\n$$\nD(y) = f_{3}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{3}y}{\\textrm d x^{3}} + f_{2}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm d^{2}y}{\\textrm d x^{2}} + f_{1}(x)\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} + f_0(x)y.\n$$\n\nwith $f_3 = x^2, f_2 = -5x, f_1 = 9,$ and $f_0 = 0.$\n\nTo compute $D(y),$ we apply the definition of the operator $D$ to the function $y\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D(y) & =D(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x})\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)-5x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)+9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}}{\\text{d}{x}^{2}}(\\frac{1}{x})-5x\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{x})+9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & ={x}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})-5x(-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(\\frac{2}{{x}^{3}})+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{x}+\\frac{5}{x}+\\frac{9}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078343 :question/id #uuid "8ddc5edb-0181-50c7-9d1a-8ee206f1c08b" :question/math-academy-id "e-6773" :question/problem "For which values of $k$ does the series $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty\\dfrac{3(2)^{kn}}{4^n}$ converge?" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the series so that it's in the form $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty a r^n,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(2)^{kn}}{{4}^{n}} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3\\frac{{({2}^{k})}^{n}}{{4}^{n}} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that this is a geometric series with a common ratio of $r = \\dfrac{2^k}{4}.$ \n\nFor the series to converge, we require $|r| < 1.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\frac{{2}^{k}}{4}| & <1 \\\\ \\frac{{2}^{k}}{4} & <1 \\\\ {2}^{k} & <4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we could remove the absolute value bars because $\\dfrac{2^k}{4}$ is always positive.\n\nThe equation $2^k = 4$ has the solution $k=2.$ Now, since $2^k$ increases as $k$ increases, the solution to $2^k < 4$ must be $k < 2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078345 :question/id #uuid "e623670b-6132-5b91-bd5e-4a52716f1d8d" :question/math-academy-id "e-689" :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\nS = \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{5}{2}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{n+1}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2+1}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{1+1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3}}{\\frac{5}{2}{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| <1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078347 :question/id #uuid "a8b61e74-1a6d-5efb-bda9-f91aca63d0d8" :question/math-academy-id "e-691" :question/problem "Determine whether the following geometric series converges or diverges.\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=0}^\\infty {3}\\left(\\dfrac{3}{2}\\right)^{n}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The series starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{1}}{3{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078348 :question/id #uuid "454e2614-06fe-5fcd-b9cd-856e544f7d1d" :question/math-academy-id "e-693" :question/problem "Which of the following geometric series converge?\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$\n\n- $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's look at each series in turn.\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 1}^ \\infty} 4 (-3)^{n}$ starts with $n=1.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}} & =\\frac{4(-3)^{2}}{4(-3)^{1}} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n = 6}^\\infty} 32 \\left(\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right)^{4n}$ starts with $n=6.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{6}} & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(7)}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4(6)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{28}}{32{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{24}} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is convergent because $|r| < 1.$\n\n- The series $\\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=0}^{\\infty}} \\dfrac{4^n}{2^{n+3}}$ starts with $n=0.$ So the common ratio is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}} & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{1+3}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{0+3}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})}{(\\frac{{4}^{0}}{{2}^{3}})} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{4}^{1}}{{2}^{4}})(\\frac{{2}^{3}}{{4}^{0}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the series is divergent because $|r| \\geq 1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only series II converges."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078357 :question/id #uuid "a57c8f1e-6fb2-5dbe-a802-166ca3082375" :question/math-academy-id "e-7356" :question/problem "Approximate the value of $\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\\right)$ using the tangent line approximation of $f(x)=\\cos{x}$ at $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}$." :question/worked-solution "The tangent line approximation of\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\cos{x}\n$$\n\nat the point\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nis\n\n$$\nL(x) = f(\\frac{π}{2}) + f^{′}(\\frac{π}{2})(x - \\frac{π}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's compute\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)\n$$\n\nin the expression above. Evaluating the function at\n\n$$\na=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\n$$\n\nwe get\n\n$$\nf\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = \\cos{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} =0\n$$\n\nNow, computing the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf^{′}(x) &= \\frac{d}{dx}(\\cos x) =-\\sin x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich in turn gives\n\n$$\nf'\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) = -\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right)} = -1\n$$\n\nSo, the tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nL(x) &= 0 - (x - \\frac{π}{2}) \\\\\n&= \\frac{π}{2} - x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate\n\n$$\n\\cos \\left(\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\\right)\n$$\n\nby evaluating the tangent line approximation at\n\n$$\nx=\\dfrac{7\\pi}{12}\n$$\n\nWe get\n\n$$\n\\cos (\\frac{7π}{12}) \\mid \\approx L(\\frac{7π}{12}); = \\frac{π}{2} - \\frac{7π}{12}; =-\\frac{π}{12}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} {:answer/id #uuid "5e7840ac-4dc2-5743-a317-909d0854aa2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273200} {:answer/id #uuid "80a9183c-594a-50ff-89ee-e5298cd3be92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "13\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273490} {:answer/id #uuid "87fc2c9b-f809-527a-9228-06e37aeb1131" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273553} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fc440f-ad27-570f-b514-432bec694cd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274578}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1deaf31a-b94f-5dbd-92e7-025ade20cd77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272654} :answer-field/id #uuid "d15396bb-b0f6-5ba4-9f17-f68ca77b61da" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8b0ed3c5-b3aa-5668-ae2c-48b159839332" :question/math-academy-id "q-15384" :question/problem "A thermometer is placed into a water bath and displays a reading of ${50}^{∘}C.$ The thermometer is then transferred to a ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath, and its reading begins to decrease. After $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the thermometer to register a reading of ${30}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the thermometer was transferred into the ${10}^{∘}C$ water bath.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={10}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-10).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-10} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-10| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-10>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-10 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-10 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-10 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =10+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${50}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=50,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}50 & =10+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 50 & =10+K \\\\ K & =40\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=10+40{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $5$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${40}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(5)=40,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}40 & =10+40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ 30 & =40{e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-5k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-5k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the thermometer reads ${30}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =30 \\\\ 10+40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =30 \\\\ 40{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =20 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the thermometer will read ${30}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})}{-\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈12\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078799 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "83f24e26-b71d-5aeb-8bcf-af942140f378" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{512}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186559345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1381eca8-6c85-572d-a31b-e3bf882f85e3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075732}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6635ce75-68d9-56d3-9e68-8210fcf2e22a" :question/math-academy-id "q-231757" :question/problem "If the fifth term of a geometric sequence is $\\frac{1}{8}$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{4},$ then the eighth term of this sequence is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{4},$ so we can start writing the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, we still need to figure out the first term ${a}_{1}.$ We know that the $5$th term is ${a}_{5}=\\frac{1}{8},$ so we can solve for the first term as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{5-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{4} \\\\ \\frac{1}{8} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{256}) \\\\ \\frac{256}{8} & ={a}_{1} \\\\ 32 & ={a}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know the first term is ${a}_{1}=32,$ we can complete the formula for the $n$th term of the sequence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can use this formula to compute the $8$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{8} & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{8-1} \\\\  & =32\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{4})}^{7} \\\\  & ={2}^{5}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{4}^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{({2}^{2})^{7}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{2}^{14}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $8$th term is ${a}_{8}=\\frac{1}{{2}^{9}}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078840 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d8553c1-2799-5628-92e5-6d9c20c2de4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "76^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075824} {:answer/id #uuid "afb0ee7a-08d1-5419-aeb0-3c605c6ab763" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075825} {:answer/id #uuid "b252be14-17c1-558f-bf5c-16bdc648313c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "44^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075826} {:answer/id #uuid "bd84efef-3f5d-5202-bd9b-6e40a7d5f991" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075827} {:answer/id #uuid "6b795f54-1aa5-52bf-9a56-f5baa81163b9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075828}] :answer-field/id #uuid "32b6aee7-8431-5086-b5d4-96bacc3456c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075823}] :question/id #uuid "44d8cbf2-6574-52ce-b3ce-66352267aa4f" :question/math-academy-id "q-27898" :question/problem "Using a calculator, find the value of $\\arccos (\\frac{1}{4})$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9cb4d2e6-902c-52a5-85b7-b7745354487c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075830} {:answer/id #uuid "ac4d59c4-d520-55fa-9969-402d5ab82c19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075831} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b856c7-0739-564d-b738-99f7ec30c69d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075832} {:answer/id #uuid "fbd3f318-b6a8-5676-abf7-528f5b26a415" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075833} {:answer/id #uuid "67e21636-62d8-509c-85fb-ed6e3d9c2ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075834}] :answer-field/id #uuid "26927dad-ceba-5c12-a274-6c1167a8cca2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186075829}] :question/id #uuid "b728edc9-2ad1-5257-9d11-6d7f8535dab4" :question/math-academy-id "q-27902" :question/problem "Find the value of $\\arctan (\\frac{1}{8})$ in degrees to the nearest degree.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c4c5325-8337-527e-9d78-2f844dff2336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076071} {:answer/id #uuid "b573706e-ea31-574e-8c9d-c1ee40cf8699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076072} {:answer/id #uuid "0510fbd7-5b24-5460-a078-bd86328fca56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076073} {:answer/id #uuid "1585debd-3902-57fe-80ec-c4cac574bf1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076074} {:answer/id #uuid "42e3379c-5177-5279-87c8-53480d3f181f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076075}] :answer-field/id #uuid "0b3ee287-3b58-5e93-b908-f21362e45be3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076070}] :question/id #uuid "a513ea40-b0dc-53b4-8d1d-73baad0553d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-386" :question/problem "What is $(\\frac{4}{5})^{-1}$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078918 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d10803c-dc1c-51df-b6d9-3387aaeb47cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495650} {:answer/id #uuid "53f63b04-ee5f-56b2-8bfc-7edc1aa13c63" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495700} {:answer/id #uuid "5c693399-2537-5d16-bc08-d75a0b068d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495722} {:answer/id #uuid "698c317a-d46e-576c-8db8-e08affaa9755" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495744} {:answer/id #uuid "ed96295d-5ba0-5de6-8e42-1e7af9fedd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496031}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed96295d-5ba0-5de6-8e42-1e7af9fedd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496031} :answer-field/id #uuid "77ddb2f4-a245-56c0-8428-a28384aa5396" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076286}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "15d287cc-b41b-5ff3-a7dc-97c2e50422d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-44320" :question/problem "Solve the equation $\\frac{8y+5}{3}=6y.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we clear the rational expression by multiplying the equation through by $3$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8y+5}{3} & =6y \\\\ 3(\\frac{8y+5}{3}) & =3\\cdot 6y \\\\ \\cancel{3}\\cdot (\\frac{8y+5}{\\cancel{3}}) & =18y \\\\ 8y+5 & =18y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve the remaining equation using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8y+5 & =18y \\\\ 8y+5-8y & =18y-8y \\\\ 5 & =10y \\\\ \\frac{5}{10} & =\\frac{10y}{10} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & =y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $y=\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078933 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "346ab41a-cd7e-5641-9b1f-158af388746d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327388} {:answer/id #uuid "7da10b9e-2f51-5fc0-bafd-506713d182bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328018} {:answer/id #uuid "95ee976a-88a3-59fc-bb0e-c1f8d48293af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328237} {:answer/id #uuid "ab4c885e-44e5-58f4-982b-238b458d7bfc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328417} {:answer/id #uuid "d6a90d39-c5c3-5906-b20c-67559555d725" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328815}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "346ab41a-cd7e-5641-9b1f-158af388746d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327388} :answer-field/id #uuid "a08d573e-b798-5da4-919c-3f1def2d4816" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329454}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b373dda-fb35-59ea-9255-3dda08116944" :question/math-academy-id "q-4487" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that as $x\\to 0,$ both numerator and denominator approach $0.$\n\nSo, if we attempt to evaluate the limit directly, we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}=\\frac{0}{0},\n$$\n\nwhich is an indeterminate form.\n\nInstead, let's recall the following special limit:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}=1\n$$\n\nRewriting the given limit using the algebra of limits and applying our special limit, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{x}{7\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} & ={lim}_{x\\to 0}(\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot \\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{x}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}{(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x})}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {[{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{x}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\cdot {1}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078994 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a72cee90-fa99-579e-ab7a-d69eeb070447" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{π\\sqrt{3}}{180}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076743} {:answer/id #uuid "f2bdf115-406d-56ae-933f-292535395733" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{π\\sqrt{3}}{180}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076744} {:answer/id #uuid "ff90fe22-0c54-5709-89fc-54759d82d3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\frac{π\\sqrt{3}}{360}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076745} {:answer/id #uuid "2e320858-44f2-5ca5-8a5e-8eba229ae040" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{π\\sqrt{3}}{360}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076746} {:answer/id #uuid "5d62b5ba-189d-546b-a0d4-fd49f5bdd5c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2} + \\frac{π}{120}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076747}] :answer-field/id #uuid "299d4319-28ca-5645-93ec-dce6c59f7a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/id